\section{Introduction to Context-Free Grammars}
\label{sec:cfg-intro}

Context-free grammars (CFGs) are the primary formalism for describing the syntax of programming languages. They generalize regular expressions by allowing recursive rules, enabling the description of nested structures such as balanced parentheses, nested expressions, and block-structured programs.

The theory of context-free grammars was developed in the 1950s by Noam Chomsky in the context of natural language grammar. John Backus and Peter Naur independently developed Backus--Naur Form (BNF) for describing the syntax of programming languages, notably ALGOL 60. The equivalence between CFGs and pushdown automata was established by Chomsky and Schützenberger (1963).

\begin{historical}
Noam Chomsky introduced context-free grammars in 1956 as part of his hierarchy of formal grammars. John Backus developed BNF in 1959 for describing ALGOL 58, and Peter Naur independently developed a similar notation for ALGOL 60. The term ``Backus--Naur Form'' was coined by Donald Knuth. The fundamental properties of context-free languages---including the pumping lemma, closure properties, and the equivalence with pushdown automata---were established in the early 1960s by Chomsky, Schützenberger, Bar-Hillel, and others.
\end{historical}

\section{Definitions and Basic Concepts}
\label{sec:cfg-basics}

\begin{definition}[Context-Free Grammar]
\label{def:cfg}
A \textbf{context-free grammar} is a 4-tuple $G = (V, T, P, S)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $V$ is a finite set of \textbf{variables} (nonterminals)
  \item $T$ is a finite set of \textbf{terminals} (disjoint from $V$)
  \item $P$ is a finite set of \textbf{productions} (rules) of the form $A \to \alpha$ where $A \in V$ and $\alpha \in (V \cup T)^*$
  \item $S \in V$ is the \textbf{start symbol}
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Derivation]
\label{def:derivation}
If $A \to \alpha$ is a production, then $\beta A \gamma \derives \beta \alpha \gamma$ for any strings $\beta, \gamma \in (V \cup T)^*$. We write $\alpha \mderives \beta$ if $\alpha$ derives $\beta$ in zero or more steps.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Language of a Grammar]
\label{def:cfg-language}
The \textbf{language} of a CFG $G$ is:
\[
L(G) = \{w \in T^* \mid S \mderives w\}
\]
A language $L$ is \textbf{context-free} if there exists a CFG $G$ such that $L = L(G)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Derivations and Parse Trees}
\label{sec:derivations-parse-trees}

\subsection{Leftmost and Rightmost Derivations}
\label{sec:leftmost-rightmost}

\begin{definition}[Leftmost Derivation]
\label{def:leftmost}
A \textbf{leftmost derivation} is a derivation in which the leftmost nonterminal is always expanded first. We write $\alpha \lderives \beta$ if $\alpha$ leftmost-derives $\beta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rightmost Derivation]
\label{def:rightmost}
A \textbf{rightmost derivation} (or \textbf{canonical derivation}) is a derivation in which the rightmost nonterminal is always expanded first. We write $\alpha \rderives \beta$ if $\alpha$ rightmost-derives $\beta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Derivation Equivalence]
\label{thm:derivation-equiv}
For every derivation $S \mderives w$, there exists a leftmost derivation and a rightmost derivation that produce $w$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Parse Trees}
\label{sec:parse-trees}

\begin{definition}[Parse Tree]
\label{def:parse-tree}
A \textbf{parse tree} (or \textbf{derivation tree}) for a CFG $G$ is a tree where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each internal node is labeled with a variable from $V$
  \item Each leaf is labeled with a terminal from $T$ or $\epsilon$
  \item If an internal node labeled $A$ has children labeled $X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n$ (left to right), then $A \to X_1 X_2 \cdots X_n$ is a production in $P$
\end{itemize}
The \textbf{yield} (or \textbf{frontier}) of a parse tree is the string of terminals at its leaves, read left to right.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Parse Tree Equivalence]
\label{thm:parse-tree-equiv}
There is a one-to-one correspondence between parse trees for $G$ with yield $w$ and leftmost (equivalently, rightmost) derivations of $w$ from $S$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Ambiguity and Disambiguation}
\label{sec:ambiguity}

\begin{definition}[Ambiguous Grammar]
\label{def:ambiguous}
A CFG $G$ is \textbf{ambiguous} if there exists a string $w \in L(G)$ that has more than one parse tree (equivalently, more than one leftmost derivation).
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Ambiguous Expression Grammar]
\label{ex:ambiguous-expr}
The grammar:
\begin{grammar}{Ambiguous Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E + E \mid E \times E \mid ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}
is ambiguous: the string \texttt{id + id $\times$ id} has two parse trees, corresponding to $(id + id) \times id$ and $id + (id \times id)$.
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Inherently Ambiguous Language]
\label{def:inherently-ambiguous}
A context-free language $L$ is \textbf{inherently ambiguous} if every CFG generating $L$ is ambiguous.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Inherently Ambiguous Language]
\label{ex:inherently-ambiguous}
The language $L = \{a^n b^n c^m \mid n, m \geq 1\} \cup \{a^n b^m c^m \mid n, m \geq 1\}$ is inherently ambiguous.
\end{example}

\subsection{Disambiguation Techniques}
\label{sec:disambiguation}

Since many programming language grammars are naturally ambiguous, we need techniques to resolve ambiguity:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Grammar rewriting}: Transform the grammar to eliminate ambiguity by encoding precedence and associativity in the grammar structure.
  \item \textbf{Disambiguation rules}: Attach precedence and associativity declarations to operators (used by Yacc/Bison).
  \item \textbf{GLR parsing}: Use a general parsing algorithm that handles ambiguity by producing all valid parse trees.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Disambiguating Expressions]
\label{ex:disambiguate-expr}
The ambiguous grammar of Example~\ref{ex:ambiguous-expr} can be rewritten as:
\begin{grammar}{Unambiguous Expressions}
  E & \longrightarrow E + T \mid T \\
  T & \longrightarrow T \times F \mid F \\
  F & \longrightarrow ( E ) \mid \textbf{id}
\end{grammar}
This grammar encodes the precedence ($\times$ binds tighter than $+$) and left-associativity of both operators.
\end{example}

\section{Normal Forms and Simplification}
\label{sec:normal-forms}

\subsection{Eliminating Useless Symbols}
\label{sec:useless-symbols}

\begin{definition}[Useless Symbol]
\label{def:useless}
A symbol $X$ is \textbf{useless} if it does not appear in any derivation of a terminal string. A symbol is useless if it is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Non-generating}: $X$ cannot derive any string of terminals
  \item \textbf{Non-reachable}: $X$ cannot be reached from the start symbol
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Phase 1:} Remove non-generating symbols
\State Initialize $\texttt{generating} = \{X \mid X \to w \text{ where } w \in T^*\}$
\Repeat
  \State Add $A$ to \texttt{generating} if $A \to X_1 \cdots X_n$ and all $X_i \in \texttt{generating}$
\Until{no change}
\State Remove all productions containing non-generating symbols
\State \textbf{Phase 2:} Remove non-reachable symbols
\State Initialize $\texttt{reachable} = \{S\}$
\Repeat
  \State Add $X$ to \texttt{reachable} if $A \to \alpha X \beta$ for some $A \in \texttt{reachable}$
\Until{no change}
\State Remove all productions containing non-reachable symbols
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Eliminating $\epsilon$-Productions}
\label{sec:epsilon-productions}

\begin{definition}[$\epsilon$-Production]
\label{def:epsilon-prod}
An \textbf{$\epsilon$-production} is a production of the form $A \to \epsilon$. A variable $A$ is \textbf{nullable} if $A \mderives \epsilon$.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Find all nullable variables
\For{each production $A \to X_1 X_2 \cdots X_n$}
  \State Add all productions obtained by omitting some subset of nullable $X_i$'s
  \State (except if all $X_i$'s are omitted, unless $A \neq S$)
\EndFor
\State Remove all $\epsilon$-productions
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Eliminating Unit Productions}
\label{sec:unit-productions}

\begin{definition}[Unit Production]
\label{def:unit-prod}
A \textbf{unit production} is a production of the form $A \to B$ where both $A$ and $B$ are variables.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State Compute the unit closure: $(A, B)$ is in the unit closure if $A \mderives B$ using only unit productions
\For{each pair $(A, B)$ in the unit closure}
  \For{each non-unit production $B \to \alpha$}
    \State Add $A \to \alpha$
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\State Remove all unit productions
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Chomsky Normal Form}
\label{sec:cnf}

\begin{definition}[Chomsky Normal Form]
\label{def:cnf}
A CFG is in \textbf{Chomsky normal form} (CNF) if every production is of the form:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $A \to BC$ where $A, B, C$ are variables ($B, C$ are not the start symbol)
  \item $A \to a$ where $A$ is a variable and $a$ is a terminal
  \item $S \to \epsilon$ (only if $\epsilon \in L(G)$)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[CNF Theorem]
\label{thm:cnf}
For every context-free grammar $G$, there exists an equivalent grammar $G'$ in Chomsky normal form.
\end{theorem}

CNF is important because it simplifies parsing algorithms: the CYK algorithm for parsing requires CNF.

\subsection{Greibach Normal Form}
\label{sec:gnf}

\begin{definition}[Greibach Normal Form]
\label{def:gnf}
A CFG is in \textbf{Greibach normal form} (GNF) if every production is of the form:
\[
A \to a \alpha
\]
where $A$ is a variable, $a$ is a terminal, and $\alpha$ is a (possibly empty) string of variables.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[GNF Theorem]
\label{thm:gnf}
For every context-free grammar $G$ (not generating $\epsilon$), there exists an equivalent grammar $G'$ in Greibach normal form.
\end{theorem}

GNF is important because it directly corresponds to a pushdown automaton that reads one input symbol per step.

\section{Properties of Context-Free Languages}
\label{sec:cfl-properties}

\subsection{The Pumping Lemma for CFLs}
\label{sec:cfl-pumping}

\begin{theorem}[Pumping Lemma for CFLs]
\label{thm:cfl-pumping}
If $L$ is a context-free language, then there exists a constant $p \geq 1$ such that every string $s \in L$ with $|s| \geq p$ can be written as $s = uvxyz$ where:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $|vxy| \leq p$
  \item $|vy| \geq 1$
  \item For all $i \geq 0$, $uv^ixy^iz \in L$
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[Non-CFL]
\label{ex:non-cfl}
The language $L = \{a^n b^n c^n \mid n \geq 0\}$ is not context-free.

\textit{Proof}: Suppose $L$ is context-free with pumping length $p$. Let $s = a^p b^p c^p$. By the pumping lemma, $s = uvxyz$ with $|vxy| \leq p$ and $|vy| \geq 1$. Since $|vxy| \leq p$, the substring $vxy$ can span at most two of the three blocks $a^p$, $b^p$, $c^p$. Pumping $v$ and $y$ will change the count of at most two symbols, making the counts unequal. Thus $uv^2xy^2z \notin L$. Contradiction.
\end{example}

\subsection{Closure Properties}
\label{sec:cfl-closure}

\begin{theorem}[Closure Properties of CFLs]
\label{thm:cfl-closure}
Context-free languages are closed under:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Union: $L_1 \cup L_2$
  \item Concatenation: $L_1 \cdot L_2$
  \item Kleene star: $L^*$
  \item Homomorphism: $h(L)$
  \item Inverse homomorphism: $h^{-1}(L)$
  \item Substitution
\end{itemize}

Context-free languages are \textbf{not} closed under:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Intersection: $L_1 \cap L_2$ (in general)
  \item Complement: $\overline{L}$
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Intersection with Regular Languages]
\label{thm:cfl-regular}
If $L$ is context-free and $R$ is regular, then $L \cap R$ is context-free.
\end{theorem}

\section{Practical Grammar Design}
\label{sec:practical-grammar}

\subsection{Designing for Parsing}
\label{sec:grammar-for-parsing}

When designing grammars for programming languages, several practical considerations apply:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Avoid ambiguity}: Use grammar structure to encode precedence and associativity, or use disambiguation rules.
  \item \textbf{Avoid left recursion}: Top-down parsers (LL) cannot handle left-recursive grammars.
  \item \textbf{Factor common prefixes}: LL parsers require that alternatives with a common nonterminal prefix be factored.
  \item \textbf{Minimize backtracking}: Design grammars that can be parsed with limited lookahead.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Common Grammar Patterns}
\label{sec:grammar-patterns}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Lists}: $L \to L , e \mid e$ (left-recursive) or $L \to e \; L' \mid \epsilon$, $L' \to , e \; L'$ (right-factored)
  \item \textbf{Optional elements}: $S \to [e] \; \ldots$ or $S \to e \; \ldots \mid \ldots$
  \item \textbf{Nested structures}: $E \to ( E ) \mid \ldots$
  \item \textbf{Precedence levels}: Use separate nonterminals for each precedence level
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Topics in Context-Free Grammars}
\label{sec:cfg-advanced}

\subsection{Linear Grammars}
\label{sec:linear-grammars}

A \emph{linear grammar} is a CFG where each production has at most one nonterminal on the right-hand side. Linear grammars generate exactly the regular languages, providing an alternative characterization of regular languages.

\begin{definition}[Linear Grammar]
\label{def:linear-grammar}
A CFG is \textbf{linear} if every production is of the form $A \to uBv$ or $A \to w$ where $u, v, w \in T^*$ and $B \in V$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Operator Grammars}
\label{sec:operator-grammars}

An \emph{operator grammar} is a CFG where no production has two adjacent nonterminals on the right-hand side. Operator grammars are important for parsing expressions because they ensure that operators are always separated by operands.

\begin{definition}[Operator Grammar]
\label{def:operator-grammar}
A CFG is an \textbf{operator grammar} if no production $A \to \alpha$ contains two adjacent nonterminals in $\alpha$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Grammar Transformations}
\label{sec:grammar-transformations}

Several transformations preserve the language of a grammar while changing its structure:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Grammar reversal}: Reverse all productions to obtain a grammar for $L^R$.
  \item \textbf{Substitution}: Replace terminals with languages (preserves CFL).
  \item \textbf{Homomorphism}: Apply a character mapping (preserves CFL).
  \item \textbf{Inverse homomorphism}: Pull back through a character mapping (preserves CFL).
  \item \textbf{Intersection with regular}: Intersect with a regular language (preserves CFL).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Decision Problems for CFLs}
\label{sec:cfl-decisions}

Several important decision problems for context-free languages are decidable:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{p{6cm}p{3cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Problem} & \textbf{Decidable?} \\
\hline
Is $w \in L(G)$? & Yes ($O(n^3)$) \\
Is $L(G) = \emptyset$? & Yes \\
Is $L(G)$ finite? & Yes \\
Is $L(G_1) \cap L(G_2) = \emptyset$? & No \\
Is $L(G_1) = L(G_2)$? & No \\
Is $L(G) = T^*$? & No \\
Is $G$ ambiguous? & No (in general) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Decision problems for context-free languages}
\label{tab:cfl-decisions}
\end{table}

\subsection{Parsing Complexity}
\label{sec:parsing-complexity}

The complexity of parsing a CFG depends on the grammar and the algorithm:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{LL(1) parsing}: $O(n)$ time, where $n$ is the input length
  \item \textbf{LR(1) parsing}: $O(n)$ time
  \item \textbf{CYK parsing}: $O(n^3)$ time for any CFG in CNF
  \item \textbf{Earley parsing}: $O(n^3)$ worst case, $O(n^2)$ for unambiguous grammars, $O(n)$ for LR(k) grammars
  \item \textbf{Valiant's algorithm}: $O(n^\omega)$ where $\omega$ is the matrix multiplication exponent
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:cfg-summary}

Context-free grammars are the foundation of syntax description for programming languages. The key concepts include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Derivations and parse trees as the fundamental semantic objects
  \item Ambiguity and its resolution through grammar rewriting or disambiguation rules
  \item Normal forms (CNF, GNF) for theoretical analysis and algorithm design
  \item The pumping lemma for proving languages non-context-free
  \item Closure properties that characterize the class of context-free languages
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The theory of context-free grammars has evolved from Chomsky's original formulation to a rich body of results and practical applications. Key milestones include: Chomsky's hierarchy (1956); BNF for ALGOL 60 (Backus and Naur, 1960); the equivalence with pushdown automata (Chomsky and Schützenberger, 1963); the CYK parsing algorithm (Cocke, 1970; Younger, 1967; Kasami, 1965); the LR parsing theory (Knuth, 1965); and the development of parser generators from Yacc (Johnson, 1975) to ANTLR (Parr, 1990s--present).
\end{historical}
